Q: C console program not producing correct output A: <p>I figured it out! There were a few issues.</p>
<h3>Snowflake Position Fix:</h3>
<ol>
<li><p>When resetting the snowflakes to the top, I set their y position to 0, but the first row is indexed at 1 in the terminal, hence why it was showing at the top for two cycles of the loop.</p>
</li>
<li><p>I should only reset the position after the y value is greater than the number of rows, not equal to it.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>Old Code:</p>
<pre><code>// Reset the position if it goes offscreen
if (sy &gt;= rows) {
    // Generate random x position
    sx = rand() % cols;
    // Reset y to the top
    sy = 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>New Code:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>// Reset the position if it goes offscreen
if (sy &gt; rows) {
    // Generate random x position
    sx = rand() % cols;
    // Reset y to the top
    sy = 1;
}
</code></pre>
<h3>Tree / Message Shifting Fix:</h3>
<p>When printing the snowflakes, each one ends with a newline character. Any snowflakes along the bottom of the screen (when I was correctly resetting the position) would add a newline that would scroll the terminal and therefore shift everything up in the display.</p>
<p>Old Code:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>// Move the cursor to the snowflake position and print it
printf(&quot;\033[%d;%dH %c\n&quot;, sy, sx, sc);
</code></pre>
<p>New Code:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>// Move the cursor to the snowflake position and print it
printf(&quot;\033[%d;%dH %c&quot;, sy, sx, sc);
</code></pre>
<p>The only caveat is that with no newline character, you need to flush stdout manually so everything appears. I did it once after the loop runs instead of doing it for each snowflake. After I loop through all the snowflakes I added this line before incrementing the index for the next generation:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>fflush(stdout);
</code></pre>

Q: Dynamic checkbox HTML generated by CodeIgniter model method only returns last iterations after looping A: <p>Because imploding single-element rows is harmless, you can unconditionally implode all of the rows while iterating. <a href="https://3v4l.org/9HsFn" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>$data = [
    ['my name', 'is', 'John'],
    ['my name is Jane']
];

var_export(
    array_map(
        fn($row) =&gt; implode(' ', $row),
        $data
    )
);
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>For the sake of demonstrating PHP8.5's pipe chaining, here's that version: <a href="https://3v4l.org/YgCZZ" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>$data = [
    ['my name', 'is', 'John'],
    ['my name is Jane']
]
|&gt;(fn($d) =&gt; array_map(fn($row) =&gt; implode(' ', $row), $d))
|&gt;var_export(...);
</code></pre>
